    "description": "Shipment Router maintains routes in a RouteGraph whose stops may be stored as adjacency maps, linked parent records, or tree nodes. Dispatch clients currently inspect those structures directly to produce manifests, calculate arrival sequences, and report accessible alternatives. The RouteGraph must retain ownership of its internal links, and clients must not depend on storage layout. A Traversal Service must expose one sequential protocol for all route representations and support depth-first, breadth-first, and ordered traversal. The traversal order is chosen per request, and each traversal must preserve its own position so that two concurrent consumers can advance independently. A traversal session receives a snapshot of the graph, exposes the next matching stop, reports completion, and invokes the caller's stop-processing callback for every result. Existing consumers need minimal changes, while adding another route representation must not require rewriting traversal loops. The team must decide where traversal state and traversal behavior should live so that clients can consume stops uniformly without learning graph internals.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose a traversal architecture that hides route storage details, preserves independent traversal state, and permits the requested traversal order to be selected per request.",
    "notes": "Iterator fits the uniform sequential protocol with per-session position over hidden route storage; strategy is the near-miss (per-request traversal order) and template-method supports the fixed visit lifecycle."
  },
